WEBVTT

00:00:00.221 --> 00:00:02.090
Here's the launcher on top of a thirty

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.562
metre cliff, firing at twenty metres per second.

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:08.087
Last time, on level ground, forty-five degrees went

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:10.752
furthest, and thirty and sixty degrees tied.

00:00:11.455 --> 00:00:13.539
Now each ball has an extra thirty metres

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.604
to fall after it comes back down to

00:00:15.665 --> 00:00:16.265
its launch level.

00:00:17.049 --> 00:00:19.432
So which of these three lands furthest from

00:00:19.473 --> 00:00:19.833
the cliff:

00:00:20.595 --> 00:00:22.758
thirty, forty-five or sixty degrees?

00:00:23.421 --> 00:00:23.806
Have a think.

00:00:27.476 --> 00:00:29.590
The level-ground shortcuts can't answer this.

00:00:30.155 --> 00:00:32.283
They came from setting the vertical displacement to

00:00:32.324 --> 00:00:34.491
zero, because the ball landed back at its

00:00:34.553 --> 00:00:35.033
launch level.

00:00:35.797 --> 00:00:38.082
Here, the sea is thirty metres below the

00:00:38.123 --> 00:00:40.668
launch point, so with up as positive, the

00:00:40.709 --> 00:00:43.936
vertical displacement at landing is minus thirty metres.

00:00:44.739 --> 00:00:46.783
Each ball reaches the launch level again just

00:00:46.824 --> 00:00:48.347
as it did on level ground, but then

00:00:48.388 --> 00:00:49.269
it keeps falling.

00:00:49.912 --> 00:00:51.798
So the flight isn't twice the time to

00:00:51.819 --> 00:00:53.967
the top any more, and the range formula

00:00:54.088 --> 00:00:54.790
doesn't apply.

00:00:55.414 --> 00:00:56.820
We go back to the two columns.

00:00:57.781 --> 00:01:00.147
The method doesn't change: find the time from

00:01:00.167 --> 00:01:02.032
the vertical column, then carry it across.

00:01:02.914 --> 00:01:04.978
Use s equals u t plus a half

00:01:04.999 --> 00:01:07.285
a t squared, with s y equal to

00:01:07.385 --> 00:01:08.167
minus thirty.

00:01:08.728 --> 00:01:10.938
That gives minus thirty equals u y t

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:12.747
minus four point nine t squared.

00:01:13.572 --> 00:01:15.715
Rearrange it, and it's a quadratic in t:

00:01:16.357 --> 00:01:18.400
four point nine t squared, minus u y

00:01:18.421 --> 00:01:20.344
t, minus thirty, equals zero.

00:01:21.128 --> 00:01:22.831
An equation with a t squared in it

00:01:23.093 --> 00:01:25.217
can have two answers, so we solve it

00:01:25.238 --> 00:01:26.320
with the quadratic formula.

00:01:27.156 --> 00:01:28.262
Let's do thirty degrees.

00:01:29.186 --> 00:01:31.732
u y is twenty sin thirty, ten metres

00:01:31.753 --> 00:01:32.153
per second,

00:01:32.695 --> 00:01:35.201
and u x is twenty cos thirty, about

00:01:35.262 --> 00:01:36.324
seventeen point three two.

00:01:37.027 --> 00:01:38.990
So the quadratic is four point nine t

00:01:39.031 --> 00:01:42.276
squared, minus ten t, minus thirty, equals zero.

00:01:43.098 --> 00:01:45.660
The formula gives t equals ten, plus or

00:01:45.701 --> 00:01:48.123
minus the square root of ten squared plus

00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:50.747
four times four point nine times thirty, all

00:01:50.808 --> 00:01:51.548
over nine point eight.

00:01:52.292 --> 00:01:54.038
That square root is the square root of

00:01:54.139 --> 00:01:56.568
six hundred and eighty-eight, about twenty-six point two

00:01:56.630 --> 00:01:56.870
three.

00:01:57.532 --> 00:01:59.635
So t is about three point seven zero

00:01:59.676 --> 00:01:59.996
seconds,

00:02:00.598 --> 00:02:02.560
or minus one point six six seconds.

00:02:03.422 --> 00:02:05.150
A negative time means before the launch.

00:02:05.672 --> 00:02:07.472
If you ran the same curve backwards, the

00:02:07.514 --> 00:02:09.074
ball would have been at sea level one

00:02:09.135 --> 00:02:11.337
point six six seconds before it was fired.

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.848
That never happened, so we reject that root.

00:02:14.633 --> 00:02:16.626
The time of flight is three point seven

00:02:16.666 --> 00:02:17.270
zero seconds.

00:02:18.197 --> 00:02:19.323
Now carry the time across.

00:02:20.025 --> 00:02:21.966
The range is u x times t:

00:02:22.547 --> 00:02:24.868
seventeen point three two times three point six

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:27.530
nine seven, about sixty-four point zero metres.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.029
Here's a quick check.

00:02:29.734 --> 00:02:31.818
The ball rises for about one point zero

00:02:31.879 --> 00:02:34.124
two seconds, to five point one metres above

00:02:34.145 --> 00:02:34.605
the cliff top.

00:02:35.107 --> 00:02:37.196
Then it falls about thirty-five metres to the

00:02:37.277 --> 00:02:39.205
sea, which takes about two point six eight

00:02:39.247 --> 00:02:39.527
seconds.

00:02:40.131 --> 00:02:42.590
Together, that's three point seven zero seconds again.

00:02:43.243 --> 00:02:44.877
The same steps work for the other two

00:02:44.957 --> 00:02:45.240
angles.

00:02:46.042 --> 00:02:48.804
At forty-five degrees, the flight lasts about four

00:02:48.865 --> 00:02:51.326
point three one seconds, and the ball lands

00:02:51.386 --> 00:02:53.228
about sixty point nine metres out.

00:02:53.928 --> 00:02:56.370
At sixty degrees, the ball stays up longest,

00:02:56.691 --> 00:02:59.152
about four point eight one seconds, but it

00:02:59.213 --> 00:03:01.614
lands only forty-eight point one metres from the

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:01.855
cliff.

00:03:02.968 --> 00:03:05.733
So from the cliff, thirty degrees goes furthest

00:03:05.814 --> 00:03:06.314
of the three,

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:09.040
and thirty and sixty no longer tie.

00:03:09.845 --> 00:03:10.307
Here's why.

00:03:10.911 --> 00:03:13.572
The extra thirty metres of fall adds time

00:03:13.633 --> 00:03:14.512
to every flight,

00:03:15.014 --> 00:03:17.734
and the flatter launches gain the most, because

00:03:17.756 --> 00:03:19.596
they come back through the launch level more

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:20.076
slowly.

00:03:20.758 --> 00:03:22.661
On top of that, a flatter launch has

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:25.447
the bigger horizontal component to spend during that

00:03:25.528 --> 00:03:26.088
extra time.

00:03:26.911 --> 00:03:29.799
So the best angle slides below forty-five degrees.

00:03:30.734 --> 00:03:31.221
Careful, though.

00:03:31.607 --> 00:03:34.434
Testing three angles doesn't prove that thirty degrees

00:03:34.575 --> 00:03:35.877
is the best possible angle.

00:03:36.681 --> 00:03:38.827
If we try many more, the range peaks

00:03:38.949 --> 00:03:41.236
at about thirty-two and a half degrees, a

00:03:41.257 --> 00:03:42.220
little above thirty.

00:03:42.943 --> 00:03:44.806
So from a height, the best angle is

00:03:44.887 --> 00:03:47.852
below forty-five, but you'd need more than three

00:03:47.913 --> 00:03:49.415
samples to find it exactly.

00:03:49.882 --> 00:03:51.498
Two slips cost marks here.

00:03:52.182 --> 00:03:54.612
One is using plus thirty for the displacement,

00:03:54.733 --> 00:03:56.440
when the sea is below the launch point.

00:03:57.243 --> 00:03:58.987
The other is doubling the time to the

00:03:59.028 --> 00:04:00.872
top, as if the ground were level.

00:04:01.515 --> 00:04:03.581
That gives about two point zero four seconds,

00:04:03.962 --> 00:04:04.624
far too short.

00:04:05.815 --> 00:04:06.855
Here's a way to remember both:

00:04:07.497 --> 00:04:09.878
down is negative, time is positive.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.141
If the landing is below the launch point,

00:04:12.683 --> 00:04:14.645
s y is negative, and of the two

00:04:14.686 --> 00:04:16.107
roots, you keep the positive one.

00:04:18.831 --> 00:04:19.355
Your turn.

00:04:20.179 --> 00:04:22.601
A ball is launched horizontally at six point

00:04:22.642 --> 00:04:25.204
zero metres per second, from five point zero

00:04:25.245 --> 00:04:26.786
metres above a level floor.

00:04:27.529 --> 00:04:30.076
Calculate its time of flight, and the horizontal

00:04:30.137 --> 00:04:31.781
distance it travels before it lands.

00:04:32.486 --> 00:04:33.337
Pause here to answer.

00:04:44.174 --> 00:04:47.476
Launched horizontally, so u y is zero, and

00:04:47.497 --> 00:04:50.519
the quadratic shrinks to minus five equals minus

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.040
four point nine t squared.

00:04:52.502 --> 00:04:54.809
So t is about one point zero one

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:55.230
seconds.

00:04:55.893 --> 00:04:58.499
The horizontal distance is six point zero times

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:00.384
that, about six point one metres.

00:05:01.287 --> 00:05:03.989
Using plus five instead gives a negative t

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.431
squared, with no real answer at all, which

00:05:06.492 --> 00:05:08.093
is your clue that the sign is wrong.

00:05:09.844 --> 00:05:10.289
Last one.

00:05:11.012 --> 00:05:13.175
A ball is launched at ten point zero

00:05:13.196 --> 00:05:15.880
metres per second, at thirty degrees above the

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.584
horizontal, from a point five point zero metres

00:05:18.765 --> 00:05:20.468
above the level ground where it lands.

00:05:21.132 --> 00:05:22.827
Calculate the time it takes to land.

00:05:23.589 --> 00:05:24.710
Pause here to answer.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:52.302
u y is ten sin thirty, five metres

00:05:52.342 --> 00:05:52.823
per second,

00:05:53.423 --> 00:05:56.605
and s y is minus five point zero,

00:05:57.005 --> 00:05:59.987
so four point nine t squared minus five

00:06:00.047 --> 00:06:02.089
t minus five equals zero.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:05.695
The quadratic formula gives about one point six

00:06:05.755 --> 00:06:06.396
four seconds,

00:06:06.957 --> 00:06:09.783
or minus zero point six two, which is

00:06:09.903 --> 00:06:10.565
before the launch.

00:06:11.249 --> 00:06:13.058
So the time of flight is one point

00:06:13.139 --> 00:06:14.064
six four seconds.

00:06:14.728 --> 00:06:16.673
Doubling the time to the top, about one

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:19.039
point zero two seconds, is the slip to

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:19.380
avoid.

00:06:21.148 --> 00:06:23.031
So when the landing is lower than the

00:06:23.092 --> 00:06:26.017
launch, solve the vertical column as a quadratic,

00:06:26.499 --> 00:06:28.923
keep the positive time, then carry it across.

00:06:29.646 --> 00:06:32.153
Here, we knew the vertical displacement and found

00:06:32.195 --> 00:06:32.615
the time.

00:06:33.218 --> 00:06:35.904
Next time, we'll know a horizontal distance instead,

00:06:36.226 --> 00:06:38.311
because a wall stands partway out from the

00:06:38.332 --> 00:06:38.612
cliff.

00:06:39.296 --> 00:06:40.122
Will the ball clear it?

00:06:42.795 --> 00:06:43.617
Here are the key notes.

00:06:44.141 --> 00:06:45.688
Pause the video if you need more time

00:06:45.729 --> 00:06:46.150
to copy them.
